rate_neutral":0.5652,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1708},{"week":18,"opponent":"GB","home":false,"pf":24.0,"pa":22.0,"off_snaps":56,"epa_per_play":-0.1703,"pass_rate":0.5357,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5349,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1059}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"NYG","distance":0.5689,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Brian Daboll"},{"season":2025,"team":"TB","distance":0.5866,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Josh Grizzard"},{"season":2025,"team":"TEN","distance":0.6215,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Brian Callahan"},{"season":2024,"team":"JAX","distance":0.6326,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"NE","distance":0.6669,"shared":21,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":0.3757,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"PHI","distance":0.4191,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jonathan Gannon"},{"season":2025,"team":"DAL","distance":0.5705,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"CIN","distance":0.5837,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6069,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Jonathan Gannon"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bears went 5-12 in 2024 and were outscored 370 to 310. The defense did most of the work, finishing 14th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 26th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 2nd highest screen rate and the 3rd lowest dime usage in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd highest screen rate and the 2nd highest no-huddle rate in the league. Caleb Williams ran the passing game and leaned heavily on D.J. Moore, who drew 27% of the targets.","defense":"The Bears defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 10th and 26th. Montez Sweat was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Darrell Taylor, who had 3 sacks while playing only 37% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 3rd lowest dime usage in the league.","special":"The Bears were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 84% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 9% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 40% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Caleb Williams played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,541 yards. D.J. Moore was the clear top target with 27% of the looks and 966 receiving yards. D'Andre Swift handled most of the work in the backfield with 74% of the carries. On defense, Montez Sweat led the team with 5.5 sacks and Jaylon Johnson picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Giants under Brian Daboll and the 2025 Buccaneers under Josh Grizzard. Their defense looked most like the 2023 49ers and the 2021 Eagles under Jonathan Gannon.","trends":"The offense went from 19th in EPA per play in 2023 to 26th in 2024, a modest decline. The defense moved from 14th in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 14th in 2024, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was pistol usage, which went from 7% to 4%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}